## Screen layout

The screen has four areas:

- **Box**: the grid of the box being filled. Filled cells show the last
  characters of the tube's ID. Parasites start with `P`. The highlighted cell
  is the next one to fill {% ref id="A" /%}.
- **Scan**: the field for scanning or typing codes {% ref id="E" /%}, or the
  work list {% ref id="C" /%}.
- **Setup** {% ref id="G" /%}: which tubes to find, the field to write, and
  the box settings.
- **Log** {% ref id="J" /%}: the tubes checked and skipped in this session.

The mode selector above the box picks what you curate.

On a phone, `Box`, `Setup`, and `Log` are tabs, and the scan field stays at
the bottom of the screen. On a tablet or desktop, the box sits on the left,
and the scan field sits on the right above the `Setup` and `Log` tabs. On a
wide desktop window, the log gets its own column.

## Set up a check

Open `Setup` before the first scan. NAHPU keeps these settings on the device,
so the next session starts where you left off.

### Find tubes

These filters decide which tubes a scan and the work list match.

- `Include parasites`: also finds parasites, by parasite ID or by the field
  ID of their host.
- `Tissue type`: the part types to match, such as Liver. Leave it at `Any` to
  match every type. Parasites have no part type, so this filter never
  excludes them.
- `Preservation`: the treatments to match, such as ETOH.
- `Parasite category`: shown with `Include parasites` on. Matches categories
  such as Ectoparasite.

### Update field

- `Field`: `Museum permanent` or `Museum loan`.
- `Value`: the code written to each tube, usually the museum's code. Scanning
  is blocked until you enter a value.

### Box

- `Box size`: 81 (9×9) or 100 (10×10) cells.
- `Fill order`: `Rows, left to right`, `Columns, top to bottom`, or
  `Serpentine rows`. In serpentine order, each row runs in the opposite
  direction of the row before it.
- `Box label` and `Box number`: name the box in the storage location, such
  as `Box 1`. Use the label for a freezer or rack prefix, such as `F2 Box`.

When a box is full, the next tube goes into the first cell of the next box.
Select `Next box` or `Previous box` under the grid to change boxes yourself.
NAHPU reads the storage location of every tube in the project, so cells
filled earlier, by this tool or typed in the part form, show as filled.

### Labels and overwrite

- `Customize cell labels`: changes how locations are written. Choose a
  `Numbering` of `A1, A2, A3…`, `1, 2, 3…`, or `1-1, 1-2, 1-3…`, a
  `Box separator`, a `Row–column separator`, and `Pad numbers with zeros`. An
  example under the fields shows the result, such as `Box 1 / A01`. Turn it
  off to go back to the standard `Box 1: A1`. NAHPU keeps your custom format
  for next time.
- `Overwrite existing values`: lets a scan replace a stored location or a
  different museum code. With it off, these tubes are reported as conflicts
  and left as they are.

## Scan tubes

Use `Scan` when tubes come to you in no set order.

{% steps %}

1. Select `Scan` {% ref id="B" /%} above the scan field.
2. Scan a tube's barcode with a barcode scanner, or type the code and press
   Enter. On a phone or tablet, `Scan with camera` reads codes with the
   device camera.
3. Check the message under the field. A checked tube shows its new location
   and value, such as `AC-T0128 → Box 1: D1 · Museum permanent = EXM`.
4. Put the tube in the cell the message names, then scan the next tube.

{% /steps %}

By default, a code matches a part's tissue ID or barcode, or a parasite ID
when parasites are included. A barcode match wins over a tissue ID match.
Suggestions appear while you type. Pressing Enter always uses the typed text,
so a scanner's code is never swapped for a suggestion.

Turn on `Search by field ID` {% ref id="D" /%} when tubes are labeled with
the specimen's field ID instead. A field ID usually matches several tubes,
such as a liver and a muscle. Add the part type or treatment after the ID to
choose one, such as `AC128 liver etoh`. A number alone, such as `128`,
matches every specimen with that number. When a code still matches more than
one tube, NAHPU asks which one you are holding. Closing that list without a
choice logs the code as skipped.

The message under the field reports each result:

| Result | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| Checked | The tube was written to the cell and the box moved on. |
| Not found | No tube matches the code and filters. No cell was used. |
| Already checked | The tube was checked earlier in this session. |
| Conflict | The tube already has a location or another museum code. Turn on `Overwrite existing values` to replace them. |
| Skipped | A choice between matching tubes was dismissed. |

`Undo` {% ref id="F" /%} reverts the most recent check. The tube gets back
its previous value and location, and its cell is free again. Select `Undo`
again to step further back.

## Work through a list

Use `Work list` when you fill a box in field ID order, such as all liver
tubes from a trip.

{% steps %}

1. Select `Work list` {% ref id="C" /%} above the scan field.
2. NAHPU lists the tubes that have no storage location and match the `Find
   tubes` filters, in field ID order. The current tube and the cell it will go
   to are shown first.
3. Select `Place` to write the tube to the next cell, or `Skip` to move past
   it. Skipped tubes stay on the list and show under `Skipped` in the log.
4. Use `Search work list` to jump to a tube by ID, barcode, type, or
   treatment.

{% /steps %}

## Work with the box

- **Change the next cell**: tap an empty cell. The next tube goes there, and
  filling continues in fill order from that cell.
- **See a filled cell**: tap a filled cell to see its tubes
  {% ref id="H" /%}. `Details` shows the tube's IDs, count, and storage
  location, and `Open record` opens its specimen form. A cell shared by
  several tubes, usually from locations typed by hand, shows how many tubes
  it holds.
- **Replace a cell**: in a filled cell, select `Replace` {% ref id="I" /%}
  and confirm. The next tube you scan or place goes into that cell, and the
  tubes that were there lose their location and go back to the work list.
  Undo puts them back.

## Session log and export

The log lists the tubes checked in this session, newest first, under
`Checked`, and the tubes passed over under `Skipped`. A skipped tube leaves
the list once it is placed.

Select `Export log` {% ref id="K" /%} to save the session as CSV, TSV, or
Excel. Each row has the curation columns first: `Status`, `Order`,
`Field ID`, `Tube ID`, `Kind`, `Type`, `Treatment`, `Updated field`, `Value`,
`Location`, `Box`, `Cell`, `Previous value`, `Previous location`, and
`Replaced`.

Under `Columns`, select `Add columns` to add part, specimen, and linked record
fields after them, such as the catalog number or species. `Added column
headers` names those columns by `Field names` or by `NAHPU keys`, such as
`specimenPart::remark`. NAHPU keeps the columns you add for the next export.

Keep the exported log with the box inventory. It records what each scan
changed, including the previous values that were replaced.
